## 15. An exact-sum Poisson formula and its rank-one contribution

Put $Q=p^3$, $q=p^2$, $B=R^2/Q$, $L=Q/R$, and let $V\asymp p^{3/2-\delta}$ be the original lower bound for the full-slope minimum. Let $S=\sum_i x_i$ and $T=\sum_i y_i$. The original word makes $S,T$ units. Write $(S,T)=h(a,b)$ where $h>0$ and $(a,b)=1$. Then $a,b,h$ are units and the full slope is $b/a\bmod Q$. Since $(a,b)$ is itself a full-slope vector, $\max(|a|,|b|)\gg V$.

For the signed estimates below, fix the possible sums of the centered representatives, so $|S|,|T|\ll Q$, and enlarge the positive count to all literal triples with these exact sums, bandlimited positive majorants of the profiles, the necessary pivot bound, and the actual middle-slope mask. This enlargement drops the full-slope minimum test from the new weighted sum while retaining the full slope congruence. The critical sector $gST=27\bmod q$ can be removed before the enlargement. In the original full-minimum range, the total row weight on any translate of a full-slope lattice is $O(B)$. With the Cauchy majorant $\phi_R(t)=(1+(t/R)^2)^{-1}$, the inequality $\phi_R(x)\phi_R(S-x)\ll\phi_R(S)(\phi_R(x)+\phi_R(S-x))$ gives a triple convolution bound $O(B^2\phi_R(S)\phi_R(T))$. The fixed-product progression $ST\equiv27/g\bmod q$ has total Cauchy weight $O_\varepsilon(p^\varepsilon R^2/q)$ by the divisor bound and dyadic summation. This costs $O_\varepsilon(p^\varepsilon pB^3)$.

